<% content_for :title, "Help — Tinkerex" %>

<div class="page-head"><h1>Help</h1></div>

<%#
  WHAT A MAKER NEEDS TO KNOW TO USE THIS SERVER, IN THEIR WORDS (onboarding, 2026-10-13; spec
  docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-13-f-onboarding-design.md). Every sentence is a claim about the
  app, so each was checked against the code or the invariants file that owns it before it was
  written here: docs/invariants/project-intake.md for Projects/ and filing,
  docs/invariants/model-intake.md for 3D models, docs/invariants/addons.md for tools,
  docs/INSTALL.md for accounts, mail and the library folder (K, 2026-10-20: Tinkerex::LibraryRoot
  and /settings/library). Change the behaviour, change the sentence.

  THE SENTENCES THAT DIFFER PER BACKEND ASK `capabilities`, never an adapter class or a backend
  name (CLAUDE.md section 14a): scannable for a library polled from a folder or a bucket,
  presignable to say bucket rather than folder, syncable for the desktop app, versioned for
  history. help_page_test.rb renders all three shapes by swapping the capabilities, because every
  leg's fixture is a share library and the other two arms would otherwise never render in a test.

  WHERE FILES COME FROM IS WRITTEN INLINE BELOW, and the same change gives the empty states a
  shared/_where_files_come_from partial saying it. Once both have merged, render that partial in
  place of the folder or bucket, sync-server and Upload paragraphs of Your library, so the two
  cannot drift apart.

  NO SETTING NAMES OUTSIDE THE LAST SECTION. The person running the server is a different reader:
  their variables and console commands sit in its collapsed details, and help_page_test.rb holds
  every other section to that.
%>
<div class="prose">
  <section aria-labelledby="library">
    <h2 id="library">Your library</h2>
    <% if capabilities.scannable? %>
      <% if capabilities.presignable? %>
        <p>Your library is a storage bucket. Anything put into it by other means appears here
          within 15 minutes, or straight away with <em>Rescan library</em> under ⋯ on the
          <%= link_to "Files", files_path %> page.</p>
      <% else %>
        <p>Your library is a folder on this server. Anything put there — over a NAS share, by a
          slicer or another program, by copying it in — appears here within 15 minutes, or
          straight away with <em>Rescan library</em> under ⋯ on the <%= link_to "Files", files_path %>
          page.</p>
        <p>The library is the whole folder the server was given, or a folder inside it, chosen in
          <%= link_to "Library folder", settings_library_path %> under Settings. Changing it keeps
          your files' projects, notes and tags when the new folder holds the same files, as when the
          old one was moved or renamed, and is refused when it holds a different set of files.
          <em>Start over with this folder</em> is for that case: it forgets every file the library
          had indexed, with their previews, notes and pins and the 3D models found among them. Your
          projects, tasks, inventory and BOMs stay, and a project keeps its folder when the new
          library has one at the same path, such as <code>Projects/Desk Lamp</code>. It deletes and
          moves nothing on disk.</p>
      <% end %>
      <p>Renaming or moving a file there keeps its notes, tags and project links, as long as its
        content has not changed.</p>
    <% end %>
    <% if capabilities.syncable? %>
      <p>Your library lives on a sync server. The desktop app keeps a folder on your computer in
        sync with it, and changes made on another device appear here within a few minutes.</p>
    <% end %>
    <p><em>Upload</em>, in the top bar, puts files in the folder you are looking at; on a page with
      no folder it asks which one.</p>
    <% if capabilities.versioned? %>
      <p>Saving over a file keeps the version before it.</p>
    <% else %>
      <p>Saving over a file replaces it: this library keeps no older versions.</p>
    <% end %>
    <p>Deleting a file or a folder moves it to <%= link_to "Trash", trash_index_path %>, where
      <em>Restore</em> puts it back. A file whose folder was deleted too comes back only after its
      folder does, so restore the folder first.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="projects">
    <h2 id="projects">Projects</h2>
    <p>A folder directly under <code>Projects/</code> becomes a project within 15 minutes, with its
      files attached. <em>New project</em> makes a project and its folder together, and
      <em>Make this folder a project</em>, under ⋯ on a folder's Files page, turns a folder
      anywhere else into one where it stands.</p>
    <p>Files dropped loose at the top of a project's folder are filed into type folders —
      <code>models/</code>, <code>docs/</code>, <code>images/</code> and so on — by the next
      sweep, following the rules in <%= link_to "Shelves", settings_shelves_path %>. A README
      stays at the top, and nothing inside a git repository is moved.</p>
    <p><em>Archive</em> puts a project away and leaves its folder where it is; the app will not make
      that folder into a project again. <em>Unarchive</em> brings it back.</p>
    <p>A project is private until you publish it from its Publish panel. Pick a handle first (also
      on your <%= link_to "Account", edit_user_registration_path %> page), then choose
      <em>Unlisted</em>, for anyone with the link, or <em>Public</em>, listed on
      <%= link_to "Explore", public_explore_path %> and on your handle's page. Visitors see the
      project page and can download every visible file in its folder and every file attached to
      it or its build log; <em>Preview</em> shows you exactly what they see.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="models">
    <h2 id="models">3D models</h2>
    <p>The <%= link_to "3D models", files_models_path %> view gathers your 3D files into models. A
      3D file loose at the top of the library, in a top-level folder, in <code>Models/</code> or in
      a project's <code>models/</code> folder is a model of its own, and a folder inside a
      top-level folder or a <code>models/</code> folder is one model holding what is inside it. A
      project's own folder is never a model, and
      removing a model from the catalog leaves its files where they are.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="tools">
    <h2 id="tools">Tools</h2>
    <p><%= link_to "Tools", tools_path %> are add-ons — small apps that run inside Tinkerex and save
      what they make straight into your library. Some need a service that the person running the
      server sets up beside it; until they do, the tool's card is greyed out and names the service
      it needs. The Box Generator needs the boxes service and OpenSCAD the openscad service;
      Generators needs none.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="account">
    <h2 id="account">Your account</h2>
    <p>The first account made on this server is its owner. After that, sign-ups are closed unless
      the person running the server opens them, and they can also add an account for you.</p>
    <p>Your email, password and public handle are on your
      <%= link_to "Account", edit_user_registration_path %> page. Tinkerex sends email, to confirm
      an account or reset a password, only once the person running the server has set up mail: if
      you forget your password and no reset email arrives, ask them to reset it.</p>
  </section>

  <details id="server">
    <summary>For the person running this server</summary>
    <p><%= link_to "docs/INSTALL.md", "https://github.com/chaim354/tinkerex/blob/main/docs/INSTALL.md" %>
      covers installing, the three library backends, mail, accounts and backups.</p>
    <p>Tools: <code>ADDON_SOURCE_URL</code> points at an addon bundle server, or
      <code>ADDON_SOURCE_PATH</code> at a directory of bundles. A tool that needs a service stays
      greyed out until <code>&lt;NAME&gt;_SIDECAR_URL</code> points at it:
      <code>BOXES_SIDECAR_URL</code>, <code>OPENSCAD_SIDECAR_URL</code>.</p>
    <p>Accounts: <code>REGISTRATION</code> is closed by default, and <code>REGISTRATION=open</code>
      is accepted only on the sync-server backend, where every account has a library of its own.
      <code>SMTP_ADDRESS</code> turns mail on. Without mail,
      <code>bin/rails 'tinkerex:owner[EMAIL]'</code> adds a confirmed account and
      <code>bin/rails 'tinkerex:password[EMAIL]'</code> resets a password, each reading the new
      password from <code>TINKEREX_PASSWORD</code>.</p>
    <p>The source is at <%= link_to "github.com/chaim354/tinkerex", "https://github.com/chaim354/tinkerex" %>.</p>
  </details>
</div>
